-### [3D Transfer Function Texture Layout](5646-3d-transfer-function-texture-layout.md)
-Defines the memory layout for hardware-based volume rendering using multi-dimensional transfer functions (MTFs). It addresses the need to pre-compute classification variables (such as gradient magnitude) at each voxel sample point, imposing specific alignment constraints on the texture memory layout to support auxiliary data volumes like normals.
+Implements volume rendering on hardware that supports the OpenGL pixel texture (dependent texture) extension, specifically targeting architectures like the SGI Octane 2 with V-series graphics cards. It solves the problem of applying multi-dimensional transfer functions (classification and shading) by using color fragments from a rendered slice to generate texture coordinates for a 3D lookup texture, effectively replacing the fragment color with the result of the transfer function evaluation. It differs from per-fragment dependent texture reads (e.g., GeForce3) by requiring an auxiliary scratch buffer for intermediate storage, as the pixel texture operation does not occur directly during rasterization on the target hardware.
 
 ### [Sparse Volume Texture (SVT)](7011-sparse-volume-texture-svt.md)
-Trades rendering speed for data compression by chunking voxel data into 3D tiles and storing only non-empty tiles in a physical texture, using a second volume texture as a page table for lookup. It circumvents API limits on maximum texture dimensions by mapping a large logical coordinate space onto a smaller physical texture, though it incurs overhead from padding and mipmap hierarchies.
+Trades rendering speed for data compression by chunking voxel data into 3D tiles and storing only non-empty tiles in a physical texture, using a second volume texture as a page table for lookup. It solves the problem of rendering large volume datasets that exceed the maximum dimension limits of standard 3D textures (e.g., 2048 voxels per dimension) while managing memory footprint. Unlike dense grids, SVT circumvents API limits by mapping a large logical coordinate space onto a smaller physical texture containing only active tiles, though it incurs overhead from padding and mipmap hierarchies.
 
 ### [Splat Texture Layout & Mapping](7089-splat-texture-layout-mapping.md)
-Stores and accesses 3D Gaussian splat data within GPU 3D textures by mapping an unstructured set of splats into a fixed-size, hardware-sampled 3D texture volume. It uses a dense, pre-allocated texture structure with specific bit-packing for coordinates and layer indices, differing from sparse volume textures by its dense allocation.
